xmlp = XML Packet
this is client/server communication over network/internet
decryption and definitions/outlines of the packets are documented in server/common/python/docs/


<elementDef>
the outermost wrapper tag around an entire XMLP class structure, close with </elementDef>

<element name="name" type="util_Rowset" />
you supply the actual object (not just a pointer) to the util_Rowset
this is used for nexted xmlp defines   mostly client and destiny packets

<elementPtr type="xmlp-type" name="sender" />
you supply a pointer to the "xmlp-type" class object instance
this is used for nexted xmlp defines   mostly client and destiny packets

<raw name="name" />
all data is determined in code
decode sets object to PyRep*
encode...if object is null, sends PyNone, else sends data as set in code

<objectEx name="name" type2="*true/false*">
i still dont fully understand what "type1/type2" is...

<objectInline>
specifies a PyObject object, closed by </objectInline>

<substreamInline>
specifies a PySubStream object - this is used by lower level marshalling/unmarshalling, closed by </substreamInline>

<none />
specifies a PyNone object

<dictInline>
wrap a dictionary in this and its closing tag </dictInline>

<dictInlineEntry key="key_ident" key_type="*int/long*" soft="*true/false*">
this defines a dictionary key as a string named "key_ident".
these are used inside <dictInline> elements
(encode - optional)key_type means this entry is an integer or long instead of a string, and encoded accordingly
(decode - optional)soft = make error note and continue on invalid decode   --is this right?

<dict name="name" />
defines a whole dictionary object of type PyDict

<dictInt name="name" />
defines a whole dictionary with integers used for ALL key values

<dictStr name="name" />
defines a whole dictionary with strings used for ALL key values

<dictRaw name="name" key="Ctype" value="Cval" pykey="Ptype" pyvalue="Pval" />
defines a whole dictionary with "Ctype" of a C/C++ type (such as uint32) as keys and value  of a C/C++ type specified by "Cval"
with the Python key type specified by "Ptype" and the Python value type specified by "Pval"
i.e. <dictRaw name="items" key="uint32" value="uint32" pykey="Int" pyvalue="Int" />

<stringInline value="value" />
specifies a constant PyString with a value of "value"

<tupleInline>
wrap a tuple in this and its closing tag </tupleInline>

<tuple name="name" />
specifies a tuple object of type PyTuple

<listInline>
wrap a list in this and its closing tag </listInline>

<list name="name" />
specifies a list of objects identified as "name"

<listInt name="name" />
specifies a list of PyInt objects identified as "name"

<listStr name="name" />
specifies a list of PyString objects identified as "name"; no explicit

<string name="name" />
simple object type PyString identified as "name"

<wstring name="name" />
simple object type PyWString identified as "name"

<real name="name" >
simple object type PyFloat identified as "name"

<int name="name" />
simple object type PyInt identified as "name"

<long name="name" />
simple object type PyLong identified as "name"

<bool name="name" />
simple object type PyBool identified as "name"


integer, decimal, string types... "none_marker" means "default" because "default" is a keyword.  :/
<*type* *name* (optional)none_marker="*value*"/>
decoding...
	none_marker means if this tag IsNone(), initialize an *name* of *type* to *value*
	if none_marker isnt used, initialize an *name* of *type* to "0".

encoding...these should never be null.  throw error and initialize a *name* of *type* to "0".


all container types...
<*type* *name* />
decoding...
	if *name* IsNone() set to PyNone() and update code to test for this....why??? it's already a none...

encoding...
	for tuple, list, dict... if *name* is empty, set to PyStatic.mt*type*()
	others... if *name* is null, set to empty*type*

**NOTE:  under no circumstances should any xmlp tags be null after encode/decode (nor should they throw errors)



